# Reverse proxy for AutomowerWeb - the only container with 80/443 published
# to the QNAP host (see docs/deployment.md / the plan in
# docs/qnap_infrastructure_setup.md for the full architecture). Caddy handles
# TLS automatically via Let's Encrypt once AUTOMOWER_HOSTNAME actually
# resolves to this NAS's public IP and 80/443 are reachable from the
# internet (both needed for the ACME HTTP challenge).
#
# Both env vars are required (no defaults) - deliberately, so a
# misconfigured container fails obviously at startup instead of silently
# proxying nowhere or serving on an unintended hostname:
#   AUTOMOWER_HOSTNAME  - the public hostname, e.g. something.myqnapcloud.com
#   AUTOMOWER_UPSTREAM  - where AutomowerWeb is actually reachable FROM
#                         INSIDE THIS CONTAINER. Not "localhost" - that's
#                         this container, not the QNAP host or the
#                         AutomowerWeb container. Use the QNAP's own LAN IP
#                         plus AutomowerWeb's published port (e.g.
#                         192.168.10.142:5152) - going back out via the
#                         host's network rather than relying on
#                         inter-container Docker networking, which wasn't
#                         verified to work on this QNAP's LXD-backed bridge.
#
# Run with a persistent /data mount (e.g. -v caddy-data:/data) - that's
# where Caddy stores its Let's Encrypt certificate/account state; without
# it, every container recreate re-requests a fresh cert and risks Let's
# Encrypt's rate limits.
{$AUTOMOWER_HOSTNAME} {
    reverse_proxy {$AUTOMOWER_UPSTREAM}

    # Cheap upgrade path if/when auth is wanted later - uncomment and set
    # AUTOMOWER_BASIC_AUTH_HASH (a bcrypt hash, via `caddy hash-password`)
    # as another env var. One line, no code change needed in AutomowerWeb
    # itself.
    # basicauth {
    #     terje {$AUTOMOWER_BASIC_AUTH_HASH}
    # }
}

# Serving a second site later doesn't need a new container, port, or
# Altibox rule - this Caddy instance already owns the only forwarded ports
# (8880/8443) and can dispatch by hostname or path to as many backends as
# wanted:
#
#   - Same hostname, different path - no new DNS name needed at all:
#       {$AUTOMOWER_HOSTNAME} {
#           handle_path /otherapp/* {
#               reverse_proxy <other-upstream>
#           }
#           reverse_proxy {$AUTOMOWER_UPSTREAM}   # falls through to this one
#       }
#
#   - Different hostname, same Caddy instance - needs a second DNS name
#     pointed at this same public IP (e.g. a hermit.no subdomain - the free
#     myQNAPcloud name is one hostname per NAS, not per app), then just
#     another site block here. Caddy requests/renews its own Let's Encrypt
#     cert per hostname automatically, no extra config beyond the block
#     itself:
#       otherapp.hermit.no {
#           reverse_proxy <other-upstream>
#       }
